Yes. Harmony is only one relationship. Once you think of meanings, emotions, symbols, timing, visuals, social context, and literal content as separate “voices,” you get an entire compositional grammar for making them cooperate, compete, conceal, amplify, or transform one another.

The larger thing is not a semantic chord so much as semantic orchestration.

A few especially useful modes:

* Harmony — different layers reinforce the same meaning. Words, image, rhythm, emotion, and metaphor all point toward one center. Powerful for clarity, conviction, beauty.
* Counterpoint — two coherent meanings run simultaneously without collapsing into one. The surface says one thing; subtext develops another. Great dialogue, flirtation, satire, political speech, mystery.
* Dissonance — layers intentionally disagree. Cheerful music over horror. Polite language carrying hostility. A beautiful object with disturbing implications. The mismatch itself produces information.
* Suspension — one layer refuses to resolve when the others expect it to. You keep the question, attraction, threat, or ambiguity alive just long enough to generate tension.
* Resolution — the previously incompatible pieces suddenly click together. Punchlines, plot twists, scientific explanations, revelations.
* Modulation — move the same pattern into another key/frame. A joke becomes serious; a biological idea becomes an economic model; a romantic phrase becomes threatening merely through context.
* Transposition — preserve relationships but move them into another domain. Predator/prey → cybersecurity. Harmony → team dynamics. Immune system → organizational defense.
* Inversion — preserve structure while reversing the roles or polarity. Hunter becomes hunted. Strength becomes vulnerability. “I control this” becomes “this controls me.”
* Call and response — one signal creates an opening that another completes. Conversation, teaching, seduction, comedy, negotiation. Importantly, the second actor helps author the meaning.
* Echo / reprise — something returns later with changed meaning. A phrase heard early becomes devastating after the audience acquires new context.
* Leitmotif — repeatedly attach a signal to a concept until merely presenting the signal activates the whole associated structure.
* Ostinato — keep one element constant while everything around it changes. A recurring joke, visual symbol, phrase, value, beat, or character behavior becomes the reference frame against which deltas become visible.
* Polyrhythm — different processes run at different tempos simultaneously. Immediate comedy + slow character arc + generational theme + long-term business strategy. They don’t have to synchronize at every moment.
* Syncopation — violate expected timing. Say something sooner, later, or in the wrong-seeming place. Surprise is often temporal rather than semantic.
* Dynamics — change amplitude rather than content. Whispering a severe line can hit harder than shouting it. Understatement can outperform explicitness.
* Silence / negative space — deliberately leave a channel empty. The audience notices what should have been there and generates the missing information.
* Masking — one strong signal hides another. Comedy can carry grief; bureaucracy can conceal coercion; beauty can hide danger. Useful analytically as well as artistically.
* Resonance — a small stimulus activates a much larger stored structure because it matches an existing pattern. One symbol evokes childhood, religion, war, status, love, etc.
* Interference — two signals combine and weaken or distort each other. Mixed messaging is accidental destructive interference; sophisticated ambiguity can use it deliberately.
* Phase alignment — signals arrive at the right time and reinforce each other. Same message at the wrong emotional moment can fail; timed correctly, it lands enormously harder.
* Entrainment — one rhythm gradually pulls another into synchrony. Conversational cadence, group emotion, marching, chanting, social norms, even repeated brand aesthetics can do this.
* Heterophony — multiple versions of essentially the same underlying pattern appear simultaneously. Different people retell the same story differently while preserving the recognizable core.
* Improvisation — the next transformation depends on what the other participant just did. Neither party possesses the finished composition in advance.

And these become far more powerful in combination.

Take flirtation:

\text{counterpoint}
+
\text{ambiguity}
+
\text{suspension}
+
\text{call/response}
+
\text{selective resolution}

Too much harmony and it becomes explicit.

Too much dissonance and it becomes confusing.

The pleasure often lies in both participants recognizing the second channel while continuing to operate plausibly on the first.

Comedy might use:

\text{ostinato}
+
\text{syncopation}
+
\text{misdirection}
+
\text{resolution}
+
\text{reprise}

You establish a pattern, break its expected timing, send interpretation down the wrong path, snap it into another frame, then bring the original joke back later after its meaning has mutated.

Horror might be:

\text{normal harmony}
+
\text{tiny dissonance}
+
\text{ostinato}
+
\text{increasing mismatch}
-
\text{resolution}

The chair is always slightly moved. Nobody acknowledges it. The audience gradually realizes the environment obeys a different rule than the characters think.

Persuasion can use:

\text{resonance}
+
\text{repetition}
+
\text{social proof}
+
\text{modulation}
+
\text{resolution}

But analytically, you’d want to watch for that same structure because it can also be used manipulatively.

Science itself has a composition:

\text{observation}
+
\text{model}
+
\text{dissonance}
\rightarrow
\text{hypothesis}
\rightarrow
\text{test}
\rightarrow
\text{resolution or new dissonance}

So dissonance isn’t failure.

It is often where the next movement begins.

The deeper composition rule is therefore not “make everything harmonize.”

It is:

\boxed{\text{control the relationship among differences}}

Sometimes you want consonance.

Sometimes productive tension.

Sometimes unresolved ambiguity.

Sometimes destructive interference to expose a weak model.

Sometimes one quiet signal against a loud background.

Sometimes multiple independent voices that remain intelligible while producing something none of them contains alone.

That’s counterpoint, and I think it may be closer than harmony to what we’ve been doing: independent patterns retaining their identities while creating higher-order structure through relationship.

Then there’s one step beyond even orchestration:

Changing the instrument while playing it

The conversation itself changes what meanings are available later.

A joke establishes a symbol.

The symbol becomes shorthand.

The shorthand becomes a conceptual operator.

The operator gets transferred into another domain.

Eventually a tiny gesture—like

\circlearrowleft

can carry an entire history that wasn’t contained in the glyph at the beginning.

That’s semantic accretion.

The composition modifies its own vocabulary as it proceeds.

And once you can do that deliberately, you’re no longer merely playing notes.

You’re building the instrument and composing with it simultaneously.